WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.115 --> 00:00:03.490
SETH VARGO: Hi
there, and welcome

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:07.090
to the fifth video in our
series on SRE and DevOps.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:09.190
My name is Seth, and
I'm a developer advocate

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:12.190
at Google focused on
infrastructure and operations.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:14.020
LIZ FONG-JONES: And
I'm Liz, an SRE focused

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:16.000
on teaching Google Cloud
customers how to build

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.145
and operate reliable services.

00:00:18.145 --> 00:00:21.280
SETH VARGO: OK Liz, so
I've defined my SLI and--

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:23.955
what are you wearing?

00:00:23.955 --> 00:00:25.330
LIZ FONG-JONES:
What do you mean?

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:27.080
The dress and this flying broom?

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:28.852
It's what we wear in the future.

00:00:28.852 --> 00:00:30.310
SETH VARGO: You're
from the future?

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:31.510
LIZ FONG-JONES: Yes.

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:33.860
Just keep going, it'll
all make sense in the end.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:35.710
SETH VARGO: OK, Liz.

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:39.040
As I was saying, I've
defined my SLIs and my SLOs

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.900
and I calculated
my error budgets.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.329
And my teams are working
together instead of fighting,

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:43.870
and I'm done!

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:47.024
I've successfully implemented
DevOps and SRE practices.

00:00:47.024 --> 00:00:48.940
LIZ FONG-JONES: But you
have customers, right?

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:51.310
SETH VARGO: I mean, we
tell our investors that.

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:53.230
LIZ FONG-JONES: So if
you have customers,

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:57.770
they might want to connect
their servers to your service.

00:00:57.770 --> 00:00:59.950
You have an API, right?

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.050
SETH VARGO:
[UNSURE MUMBLING NOISES]

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:03.454
Maybe?

00:01:03.454 --> 00:01:04.870
LIZ FONG-JONES:
Well, now you have

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:08.060
to teach your customers
how to do DevOps and SRE.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.840
SETH VARGO: Wait, so you're
saying that all of the stuff

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.910
we've talked about in
all of these videos,

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:15.970
I have to go teach
that to my customers?

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:17.470
LIZ FONG-JONES:
Yes, yes, you do.

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:20.890
SETH VARGO: But all of those
things like toil and error

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:23.770
budgets and SLIs and
SLOs-- all of that?

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:26.440
LIZ FONG-JONES: Well, you
technically don't have to,

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.690
but have you ever had
a user be angry at you

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:30.370
because your service is down?

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:31.510
Yeah.

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:34.240
Well, what happens if
your service behaves

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.660
in a way that's in spec for
your beautifully-crafted SLOs,

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:40.390
but that breaks the
customer expectation?

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:41.900
SETH VARGO: Yeah,
I can see that,

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:44.380
and no one likes unhappy users.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:45.582
We certainly don't.

00:01:45.582 --> 00:01:48.040
LIZ FONG-JONES: And you can
imagine that at Google's scale,

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:50.470
that with that
many unhappy users,

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:52.550
we'd want to do
something about it.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:54.640
So anytime you have
a consumable service

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:57.250
that has an API,
that's going to happen.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:00.460
And the best way to make for
your customers to be happy

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:04.570
is to communicate clearly about
how your service is intended

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:07.690
to behave, which
coincidentally means

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:09.091
exposing your SLOs to them.

00:02:09.091 --> 00:02:10.090
SETH VARGO: Interesting.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:12.520
So I have to teach
them how to make

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.780
a good SLO for their
systems, and that

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.220
will teach them how to interact
correctly with my system?

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:19.260
LIZ FONG-JONES: Exactly.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:22.477
SETH VARGO: OK, but
I'm not sure I follow.

00:02:22.477 --> 00:02:23.560
How is that going to help?

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.690
It seems like a lot
of upfront work.

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:27.850
LIZ FONG-JONES: So
once upon a time,

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:30.910
we didn't talk
about SRE publicly.

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:32.860
And we thought of it
as our secret sauce,

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:34.390
and we tried to keep it secret.

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:36.730
And then we learned
a very hard lesson--

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:41.050
our customers had problems,
and they had problems

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:43.060
because they had used
our systems in ways

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:45.790
that we weren't expecting
and that neither of us

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:46.960
were communicating about.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:50.410
We didn't tell our customers
how our systems were designed,

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:53.080
and they didn't tell us
what they were expecting.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:57.700
And then when everything
broke, they got very upset,

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:00.580
and you can imagine that
multiplied by millions

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:01.940
or billions of users.

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:02.980
SETH VARGO: Well, I
mean, at Google's scale,

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.370
I can imagine how that would
quickly add up over time.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:08.110
LIZ FONG-JONES: Yep, it sure
did, and we learned eventually

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:11.260
that we can move faster and
ship features to our customers

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:13.210
faster if we're
openly communicating

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:16.690
with our customers and
helping them do SRE.

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:18.580
So the time that we
spend teaching people

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:20.330
about how to do SRE--

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:21.910
why is it paying off a lot?

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:25.090
Because when users apply
these things to their systems,

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:27.530
it makes them have much
less friction with us.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:28.280
SETH VARGO: I see.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:30.820
So an initial,
upfront investment

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:34.350
on education around SRE
has long-term dividends?

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:35.350
LIZ FONG-JONES: Exactly.

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.330
And it was so
successful that now we

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:39.430
have an entire team,
which is the team that I'm

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:41.800
part of-- the Customer
Reliability Engineering Team,

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.300
or CRE.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:47.800
And CREs are regular Google SREs
that are focused externally.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:51.280
Instead of working on
our own GCP applications,

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:54.250
we instead help customers
who are implementing

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:55.930
their applications
on GCP, and we

00:03:55.930 --> 00:04:00.160
help them also implement SRE
practices like SLIs and SLOs.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:02.050
And that means that
in the long-term,

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:03.400
we can share information.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:05.770
We can share our SLOs
with our customers

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:08.470
and have our customers
share their SLOs with us.

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:11.125
So we'll know if we break
one of our customers,

00:04:11.125 --> 00:04:13.750
or our customers can figure out
whether an issue they're seeing

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:15.850
is a glitch on their
end or whether it's

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:17.496
something that's in GCP.

00:04:17.496 --> 00:04:18.620
SETH VARGO: That's so cool!

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:19.760
Thanks, future Liz!

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:22.520
[SCI-FI TELEPORTATION SOUNDS]

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:26.402
That was weird.

00:04:26.402 --> 00:04:27.788
[RUNNING FOOTSTEPS]

00:04:27.788 --> 00:04:28.972
[PANTING]

00:04:28.972 --> 00:04:30.680
LIZ FONG-JONES: I'm
sorry I'm late, Seth.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:31.965
Everything OK?

00:04:31.965 --> 00:04:33.590
I hope I didn't throw
off the schedule.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:34.540
SETH VARGO: Not a problem.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.650
We were just talking to a
future version of yourself.

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:38.120
LIZ FONG-JONES: Wait,
a future version of me?

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:38.840
That's so cool!

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:42.030
So you're saying future
me invented a time machine

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:45.290
and then went back in time so
as to make me not late to this?

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:46.124
SETH VARGO: Exactly.

00:04:46.124 --> 00:04:47.581
LIZ FONG-JONES:
That's really cool!

00:04:47.581 --> 00:04:48.380
What did she say?

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.921
SETH VARGO: So we were talking
about the Customer Reliability

00:04:50.921 --> 00:04:52.880
Engineering program--
or the CRE program--

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:55.070
here at Google, but I'm
still a little bit unclear

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:57.590
about how it plays into
this bigger DevOps picture.

00:04:57.590 --> 00:04:58.590
LIZ FONG-JONES: Ah, yes.

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:01.910
So CRE is Customer
Reliability Engineering,

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:05.270
and we focus on breaking down
the organizational barriers--

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:08.040
not between individual
silos in a single company,

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:11.570
but instead between a Cloud
customer and its platform

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:12.770
provider.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:15.950
And we also think about,
how do we make failure

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:17.750
a normal, accepted thing?

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:20.240
So for instance, we
make sure that everyone

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:22.100
has service-level
objectives and error

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:24.050
budgets, so we
communicate about what's

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:25.919
an acceptable level
of reliability

00:05:25.919 --> 00:05:27.710
so that everyone doesn't
panic when there's

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:29.510
a little bit of downtime.

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:32.450
And also, we focus a
lot on measurement.

00:05:32.450 --> 00:05:35.480
So we make sure that everyone
has visibility into the system

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:38.510
so that customers can see how
the platform is performing,

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:42.230
and the platform can see how the
customer service objectives are

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:43.830
faring during an outage.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:47.030
So in that way, we have a single
pane of glass between everyone.

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:47.780
SETH VARGO: Great.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.010
Thanks, Liz.

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:51.360
And thank you,
everyone, for watching.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.060
Don't forget to
subscribe to the channel

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:55.010
and stay tuned for
future videos--

00:05:55.010 --> 00:05:56.834
and maybe a future Liz.

00:05:56.834 --> 00:05:59.000
Check out the description
below for more information

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.420
about Customer Reliability
Engineering at Google.

